# ProgramOS instance settings.
#
# Every key here must be named in AGENTS.md or docs/setup-reference.md
# (a test checks it). docs/setup-reference.md has one table of all keys.
#
# HOW SETTINGS ARE READ (two ways; this matters):
#   1. Keys in PART 1 are read from the file `.env` in the repo root.
#   2. Keys in PART 2 are read ONLY from the process environment. A line in
#      `.env` does nothing for them. Export them in the service definition:
#      systemd `Environment=` lines (the supported recipe, docs/deployment.md),
#      or your shell before `node dist/index.js`.
#      systemd reads a backslash and `%` in an `Environment=` line itself:
#      it drops a line with `\.`, turns `\s` into a space, and refuses an
#      unknown `%` sequence. So write a pattern with neither: `[.]` for a
#      dot, a character class instead of `\s` or `\w`.
# The parser does NOT strip inline comments. Put nothing after a value.
# Never commit `.env`.

# ============ PART 1: goes in `.env` ============

# REQUIRED. The bot's name. The code default is the upstream framework's
# default name, not "Coordinator".
ASSISTANT_NAME=Coordinator
# REQUIRED. The address the bot sends from and replies to.
SEND_FROM_EMAIL=
SEND_FROM_NAME=Coordinator
# The operator's address for host mail copies. With ADMIN_ALERT_ROUTE=email
# this is also the one address operator alerts are mailed to: exactly one
# address, a person's mailbox, never the bot's own address.
OPERATOR_CC_EMAIL=
# Where operator alerts go (delivery failures, gate refusals, routed-approval
# notices, sender caps, protected-path pushes): telegram (the default when
# this line is absent), email (to OPERATOR_CC_EMAIL), teams (a Microsoft
# Teams channel, see below), or several joined by commas (telegram,email,
# email,teams, ...). With several, an alert counts as delivered when one
# route delivered it.
# At startup the bot logs one error for each setting an enabled route lacks.
# ADMIN_ALERT_ROUTE=telegram
# SECRET. With ADMIN_ALERT_ROUTE including teams: the HTTP POST URL of a
# Teams Workflows webhook ("anyone" may trigger it). The URL is the
# credential: anyone holding it can post to the channel. Read from .env
# only and never logged. Not verified against a live Teams tenant; see
# docs/teams-alerts.md.
# ADMIN_ALERT_TEAMS_WORKFLOW_URL=
TZ=America/Chicago

# Identity (src/instance-identity.ts). Each default is this repo's
# placeholder, so set all four for a real deployment. A malformed value stops
# the host at startup. With a real program repo, so does a missing or
# placeholder INSTITUTION_DOMAIN or ADMIN_AUTHOR_EMAILS
# (src/identity-settings-check.ts). Lower case; no quotes needed.
# REQUIRED for a real program repo: the host refuses to start when this is
# empty, example.edu or a subdomain of example.edu (mail.example.edu).
# Your institution's mail domain. A blocked sender on this domain gets one
# "access closed" reply, and a route named in an allowlist row resolves to
# <name>@<this domain>.
INSTITUTION_DOMAIN=
# The bot's own mail domain: the default bot address (program@<domain>, used
# when SEND_FROM_EMAIL is empty), the Message-ID domain of outbound mail, and
# the bot's git author (program-bot@<domain>). The prompts in groups/ and the
# skills in container/skills/ also name that git author as text; change them
# to match.
BOT_MAIL_DOMAIN=
# The git author email of your inbound mail intake service. It must equal the
# author on that service's commits exactly. An original .eml is accepted only
# when this author first committed it.
INTAKE_GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=
# REQUIRED for a real program repo. The admin's git author emails, separated
# by commas: the only identities allowed to change the protected governance
# files. This list replaces the placeholder list. With a real program repo
# (PROGRAM_ORIGIN_PATTERN is set, or the origin of the checkout or the read
# mirror is not example-org/program-repo) the host refuses to start when this
# is empty or names a placeholder address.
ADMIN_AUTHOR_EMAILS=

# REQUIRED. Shared secret for inbound mail webhooks.
WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Recommended. The key that signs the saved state of a reply that is part-way
# through delivery (src/delivery-state.ts). A long random value of its own:
# `openssl rand -hex 32`. Read from the process environment first, then
# `.env`, once at start. Without it the host signs with the first one set of
# WEBHOOK_SECRET, TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN and RESEND_API_KEY, and warns at each
# start: a leak of that secret then also lets its holder forge delivery
# state, and rotating it makes a reply that is in delivery at that moment
# fail its check (it is dead-lettered and the message is retried). State
# signed before this key was set still loads.
# DELIVERY_STATE_SIGNING_KEY=

# Optional. This file holds every secret, so only its owner should be able to
# read it: `chmod 600 .env`. At each start the host logs an error when the
# group or others can read it. Set this to `true` to make the host refuse to
# start instead. Read from the process environment first, then `.env`.
# REQUIRE_PRIVATE_ENV_FILE=true

# Model credentials for the agent (through the host's credential proxy).
# Use ONE of the first two. Leave unused lines out of `.env` entirely.
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Optional: an approved endpoint that serves Claude. The agent uses it, and
# so does the reply sensor when its provider is anthropic (see SENSOR_PROVIDER
# below); the sensor posts to the same host. Unset: api.anthropic.com.
# A path prefix in this URL is kept: https://gateway.example.edu/claude
# receives requests at /claude/v1/messages.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=

# Model providers for the agent, in order. Values: anthropic, foundry.
# Absent = anthropic only. `anthropic,foundry` makes Claude on Microsoft
# Foundry (Azure) the backup; `foundry,anthropic` makes it the primary.
# A provider listed here without its settings stops the host at startup.
# WARNING: every provider listed receives your team's message text. Each
# Foundry deployment has a hosting option: "Hosted on Azure" (processed and
# stored on Azure) or "Hosted on Anthropic". Check it for each deployment
# before you tell anyone where data goes. See docs/model-providers.md.
# MODEL_PROVIDERS=anthropic,foundry
# The Foundry resource name only, or the full base URL (one of the two).
# ANTHROPIC_FOUNDRY_RESOURCE=
# ANTHROPIC_FOUNDRY_BASE_URL=https://YOUR-RESOURCE.services.ai.azure.com/anthropic
# The Foundry key. It stays on the host; the container gets a placeholder.
# ANTHROPIC_FOUNDRY_API_KEY=
# Header for the key when the caller sent none: api-key (default) or x-api-key.
# FOUNDRY_KEY_HEADER=api-key
# Deployment names (name each deployment by its model ID). On a Foundry run
# the first two replace NANOCLAW_AGENT_MODEL and NANOCLAW_GATE_MODEL; the
# other three pin the aliases opus, sonnet and haiku.
# FOUNDRY_AGENT_MODEL=
# FOUNDRY_GATE_MODEL=
# FOUNDRY_OPUS_MODEL=
# FOUNDRY_SONNET_MODEL=
# FOUNDRY_HAIKU_MODEL=
# Failures in a row before a provider is set aside (default 3), and for how
# long (default 1800000 = 30 minutes).
# MODEL_FAILOVER_THRESHOLD=
# MODEL_FAILOVER_COOLDOWN_MS=
# The reply sensor's destination: anthropic or foundry. Unset, it follows the
# first provider in MODEL_PROVIDERS (anthropic when that is unset). Setting
# it overrides that. It does not fail over. With foundry, SENSOR_MODEL
# (PART 2) is a deployment name. This key is also read from the process
# environment.
# SENSOR_PROVIDER=
# The reply sensor checks chat replies (Telegram and web chat) only. Set this
# to 1 to have it also check each delivered email reply, once, after the mail
# is sent. It then sends the text of every email reply, the first 4,000
# characters of the mail it answers and knowledge-base excerpts to the
# sensor's provider: get that approved first. Mail the bot sends on its own
# (the send_email tool, scheduled mail, operator alerts) is never checked.
# BEHAVIOUR_SENSOR_DISABLED=1 (PART 2) still turns the sensor off. This key
# is also read from the process environment. Unset: off.
# SENSOR_EMAIL_REPLIES=

# Which service delivers the bot's mail: resend (the default when this line
# is absent; an HTTP API, needs DNS records on a domain you control) or smtp
# (authenticated SMTP submission, for example your institution's own mail
# system). Any other value stops the host at startup. This key is also read
# from the process environment. AGENTS.md, choice 6.
# OUTBOUND_MAIL_PROVIDER=resend
# Outbound mail provider key (the reference deployment's provider, resend).
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# For OUTBOUND_MAIL_PROVIDER=smtp. The submission server's host name only
# (no scheme, no port).
# SMTP_HOST=
# How the session is encrypted: starttls (the default; port 587) or tls
# (TLS from the first byte; port 465). There is no unencrypted mode: any
# other value stops the host at startup.
# SMTP_SECURITY=starttls
# Only when the server uses a port other than 587 (starttls) or 465 (tls).
# SMTP_PORT=
# The login for the mailbox the bot sends as.
# SMTP_USERNAME=
# Its password or app password. A secret: read from `.env` only, never from
# the process environment.
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# Optional. The SMTP envelope sender (where bounces go). Unset: the same as
# SEND_FROM_EMAIL. Leave unset unless the mail administrators give you one.
# SMTP_ENVELOPE_FROM=
# Signing secret of the provider's events webhook (starts with whsec_).
# Endpoint: https://<your-host>/resend/webhook, subscribed to email.delivered,
# email.bounced, email.complained and email.failed. Read from `.env` only.
# Without it the route answers 503 and bounces are not reported.
# Not used with OUTBOUND_MAIL_PROVIDER=smtp: SMTP has no events webhook.
# Steps: docs/setup-reference.md, "Webhooks from other services".
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Telegram. Optional as a channel for the team. Operator alerts are sent to
# the admin here unless ADMIN_ALERT_ROUTE says otherwise. With the telegram
# route and no token, those alerts are not delivered.
# Create the chat sender allowlist first (copy
# config-examples/sender-allowlist.json to ~/.config/nanoclaw/): if that file
# is missing or invalid, the chat trigger check allows no sender.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# TELEGRAM_ADMIN_CHAT_ID=

# Web chat (optional).
# WEBCHAT_SECRET=

# Web search and web fetch for the agent. Off unless set to 1. A web search
# sends the agent's query to the model provider even when the container's
# network is restricted. Read from the process environment first, then `.env`.
# While it is off, the credential proxy refuses any request that would make
# the model provider contact an outside host for the agent: a web search or
# web fetch tool, a remote MCP server, an image or document given as a URL,
# the managed agents API. It also refuses a model request it cannot read as
# JSON, and every path except the model paths, the model list and the OAuth
# key exchange. A tool type it does not know is forwarded with a warning in
# the log. A refusal is logged on the host with its reason.
# AGENT_WEB_TOOLS=1

# What the agent container can reach: restricted or open. Read from the
# process environment first, then `.env`.
#   restricted  an internal Docker network: the host's credential proxy and
#               nothing outside. The default on Docker Engine on Linux.
#   open        any host. The default on Docker Desktop (macOS, WSL), where
#               restricted is not verified. The host warns at each start.
# The knowledge base and the mail the agent reads can leave over an open
# network without passing the delivery gate or the audit log.
# IMPORTANT: on the restricted network the container still reaches any host
# service that listens on all interfaces, and the webhook listener does that
# by default. Set WEBHOOK_HOST=127.0.0.1 (below). The host warns at each
# start while the network is restricted and WEBHOOK_HOST is not loopback.
# Check with: npx tsx scripts/check-container-egress.ts
# CONTAINER_NETWORK=restricted

# Optional features.
# Secret of the push webhook on the PROGRAM repo (GitHub: Settings, Webhooks).
# Payload URL https://<your-host>/api/github-push, content type
# application/json, the push event only. Choose a long random value and enter
# the same value in GitHub. Read from `.env` first, then the process
# environment. Without it the route answers 503, direct commits get no audit
# entry and the protected-file tripwire does not run.
# Steps: docs/setup-reference.md, "Webhooks from other services".
# GITHUB_PUSH_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# EMAIL_OUTREACH_TASK_ID=
# DEAD_LETTER_ALERT_JID=
# OUTREACH_SUMMARY_JID=
# OUTBOUND_EMAIL_STAGGER_MS=

# Retention (src/retention.ts). Each value is a whole number of DAYS. 0, or
# no line, keeps everything, which is what the code did before these keys
# existed. Above 0, the host deletes what is older than that many days, once
# shortly after it starts and then once a day. Read from the process
# environment, then `.env`. A negative number, a fraction or text stops the
# host at startup; the shortest window is 1 day. Something a pending
# operation still needs is kept whatever its age. These keys do not touch the
# program repo or the audit log: no code deletes there
# (docs/data-handling.md, "Retention and deletion").
# See what a value would delete before you set it:
#   RETENTION_MESSAGES_DAYS=180 npx tsx scripts/retention-prune.ts
# The `messages` table: full text of every chat message, mail prompt, reply.
# RETENTION_MESSAGES_DAYS=0
# Routed approvals that are closed or removed: their content is cleared
# (sender, approver, subject, quoted words). The row stays, so a reopened
# entry is not mailed to the approver again.
# RETENTION_ROUTED_APPROVALS_DAYS=0
# Finished one-time scheduled tasks, and the record of each task run
# (never under 7 days, which the exceptions digest reads).
# RETENTION_TASKS_DAYS=0
# Transcript copies in groups/<group>/conversations/. The agent can no
# longer search the ones deleted.
# RETENTION_TRANSCRIPT_ARCHIVE_DAYS=0
# Telegram documents, their extracted text, and resized photos.
# RETENTION_UPLOADS_DAYS=0
# Entries in the working-context archive files. Entries are archived after
# 14 days, so a value of 14 or less empties the archive.
# RETENTION_WORKING_CONTEXT_ARCHIVE_DAYS=0
# Reply scores in store/evals.db (never under 7 days, which the weekly
# quality report reads).
# RETENTION_EVALS_DAYS=0
# Container requests the host set aside in data/ipc/errors/.
# RETENTION_IPC_ERRORS_DAYS=0

# ============ PART 2: export in the process environment ============

# REQUIRED. Absolute path to the program repo checkout on this host. The host
# pulls and pushes it with its own git credential (AGENTS.md, "First start");
# the agent container gets no git credential.
# This is also the code default, the path in `seed-db.cjs` and the path in
# `config-examples/mount-allowlist.json`. Change all of them together.
# PROGRAM_REPO=/srv/program-repo
# Optional bare mirror for pinned host reads.
# PROGRAM_READ_MIRROR=
# REQUIRED on a new host: the git origin of the program repo, as the host
# should fetch and push it. The host never takes this address from the
# checkout, whose git config the agent can write. Without it the host uses
# the origin of the read mirror, if that exists; with neither it commits the
# audit log locally and pushes nothing. Must match PROGRAM_ORIGIN_PATTERN.
# An SSH deploy key (the form below) keeps every secret out of this value.
# With an HTTPS URL that holds a token ("https://<token>@host/..."), the host
# stores and runs git with the URL without the token, and gives git the token
# through the git process's environment only: it is in no git config file and
# on no command line (src/origin-credential.ts). The token is then in this
# setting alone, so the file that sets it (the systemd override.conf) must be
# readable by its owner only (chmod 600).
# PROGRAM_ORIGIN_URL=git@github.com:your-org/your-program-repo.git
# Optional. The host's own repository: the audit log is committed here and
# every fetch and push runs here (src/host-repo.ts). Default:
# "program-repo-host" in the folder of the read mirror
# (/srv/repos/program-repo-host). The host creates it at the first start.
# It must not be inside PROGRAM_REPO, and no container may mount it. A
# second folder, "<this path>-export.git", is created beside it. The host
# keeps this folder readable by its own user only (mode 0700).
# PROGRAM_HOST_REPO=
# REQUIRED if your program repo is not the placeholder one: a regular
# expression that the repo's git origin URL must match.
# PROGRAM_ORIGIN_PATTERN=^git@github[.]com:your-org/your-program-repo([.]git)?$
# Any git host works, including GitHub Enterprise on your institution's own
# host name. When this is set, the URL is checked against the pattern only,
# so keep ^ and $. A GitHub Enterprise host, with the dots written as [.]:
# PROGRAM_ORIGIN_PATTERN=^git@git[.]example[.]edu:your-org/your-program-repo([.]git)?$
# For an HTTPS origin, with or without a token in the URL. The token part
# allows letters, digits and _ . ~ : - only, which covers GitHub tokens:
# PROGRAM_ORIGIN_PATTERN=^https://([A-Za-z0-9_.~:-]+@)?git[.]example[.]edu/your-org/your-program-repo([.]git)?$

# Only used when the host process runs as root (not the documented recipe).
# Host git and file operations in the program repo then run as this user,
# never as root. Defaults: 1000, 1000, /home/ubuntu. The agent container
# still runs as user 1000 in that case, so the checkout must stay writable
# by user 1000. A value that is not a positive whole number stops the start.
# CHECKOUT_OWNER_UID=1000
# CHECKOUT_OWNER_GID=1000
# CHECKOUT_OWNER_HOME=/home/ubuntu

# Keep the listener on loopback and put a reverse proxy in front.
# The code default is all interfaces, and a listener on all interfaces is
# reachable from the agent container even with CONTAINER_NETWORK=restricted.
# WEBHOOK_HOST=127.0.0.1
# WEBHOOK_PORT=3003
# CREDENTIAL_PROXY_PORT=3001
# Name of the internal Docker network the host creates for
# CONTAINER_NETWORK=restricted. Change it only if the name is taken.
# CONTAINER_NETWORK_NAME=nanoclaw-agents
# Address the credential proxy binds to. Used on the open network only; on
# the restricted network the proxy binds to that network's gateway address.
# CREDENTIAL_PROXY_HOST=

# The reply sensor calls a model API directly, with its own key, and sends
# reply text and knowledge-base excerpts. It does not go through the host's
# credential proxy and never fails over. Its destination follows the agent's
# first provider (MODEL_PROVIDERS) and, on anthropic, ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL;
# SENSOR_PROVIDER (PART 1) overrides the provider. The host logs the
# provider and host it uses. Keep it off unless that destination is approved
# for your data. While on, it checks chat replies only, unless
# SENSOR_EMAIL_REPLIES (PART 1) is set.
# BEHAVIOUR_SENSOR_DISABLED=1
# SENSOR_MODEL=

# First start only: send one test alert on each enabled ADMIN_ALERT_ROUTE
# route at startup and log each result. Remove it after the test.
# ADMIN_ALERT_TEST=1

# NANOCLAW_AGENT_MODEL=
# NANOCLAW_GATE_MODEL=
# NANOCLAW_AGENT_EFFORT=
# EMAIL_SENDER_DAILY_CAP=
# EMAIL_PERSON_SENDER_DAILY_CAP=

# Sender authentication for inbound mail (docs/setup-reference.md, "Sender
# authentication for inbound mail"). Process environment, then .env; read once
# at start. A value the host does not know stops the start, and the start log
# says which mode is in effect.
# flag (default): the verdict is written to the audit log, nothing else
# changes. enforce: allowlisted mail that is not authenticated is dropped.
# EMAIL_AUTH_MODE=flag
# The mail servers whose results count, by the name each writes first in its
# Authentication-Results header, comma-separated. Empty: every verdict is
# "unknown". The host does not start with enforce and this empty. The intake
# worker has matching settings (inbound/wrangler.toml).
# EMAIL_AUTH_TRUSTED_AUTHSERV_IDS=
# enforce only: block (default) drops a message with the verdict "unknown";
# allow processes it. "unknown" means no server was asked, or the mail platform
# wrote no result; a result that was there and could not be relied on is
# "unauthenticated".
# EMAIL_AUTH_UNKNOWN=block
# true (default): a DMARC pass counts only when the server names the From
# domain it checked (header.from=...). false: a pass from the mail platform
# that names no domain counts for the From address of the message. Set false
# only after the offline check of a real message shows your platform writes no
# domain (inbound/README.md, "Check one real message offline").
# EMAIL_AUTH_REQUIRE_FROM_DOMAIN=true
